1. e4 c5 2. c3 b6 ({I've played this before against Paul. In the morning I had glanced at} 2... Nf6 {, but when it came to it I just had to play ...b6.}) 3. d4 Bb7 4. Bd3 (4. d5 {is possible.}) 4... cxd4 $6 {Extremely dubious.} 5. cxd4 Nc6 6. Nf3 (6. d5) 6... Nb4 $5 7. Bc4 $5 ({White can just play} 7. Nc3 Nxd3+ 8. Qxd3 {, when he has a considerable advantage in development for the two bishops.}) 7... Rc8 8. Ne5 e6 (8... Rxc4 $5 {certainly came into consideration.}) 9. Nc3 f6 $5 ({Black gets some play, but probably not enough.} 9... Bd6 {might have been more sensible (} 10. Nb5 $5 Bb8 $1 {)}) 10. Qh5+ g6 11. Nxg6 hxg6 12. Qxh8 Kf7 13. Qh7+ $5 {This could have been omitted.} Bg7 14. Bb3 ({I was more worried about} 14. Nb5 d5 15. Bf4 {but} Rc6 {seems very adequate.}) 14... Nd3+ 15. Ke2 Ba6 16. Kf3 Nxc1 {A bit disagreeable, but I couldn't find anything better.} 17. Raxc1 Nh6 18. Nd5 $6 (18. h4 $1 {leaves White with the advantage, for} f5 19. h5 Rxc3+ 20. Rxc3 fxe4+ {just doesn't work after e.g.} 21. Kxe4 d5+ 22. Kf3 Qf6+ 23. Kg3 Qg5+ (23... Nf5+ 24. Kh2 {, etc}) 24. Kh2 {and Black runs out of checks.}) 18... f5 19. Rxc8 $4 ({White should play} 19. Nf4 Qg5 20. Qxg6+ {with an unclear ending probably quite good for him.}) 19... fxe4+ ({Not} 19... Qh4 20. Rf8+ $3 {(the only move)} Kxf8 21. Qxg6 {defending.}) 20. Kxe4 Qh4+ 21. f4 ({When playing 9 Rxc8, Paul had missed that} 21. Nf4 {can be met by} d5+ {, which wins.}) 21... Bxc8 {And Black is winning.} 22. Ne3 Qf2 23. Rc1 {Other moves also lose.} d5+ 24. Nxd5 ({Or} 24. Bxd5 exd5+ 25. Kxd5 Bb7+ 26. Kd6 Qxf4+ {, winning.}) 24... Qe2+ 25. Ne3 Bb7+ 26. d5 exd5+ 27. Bxd5+ Bxd5+ 28. Kxd5 Qd2+ $1 {The simplest.} 29. Ke4 Qxc1 30. g4 Qc6+ 31. Kd3 Nxg4 32. Nxg4 Qd7+ {Sources: BCM, November 1978, p484; New Chess Player, Vol.4/1978/D, p111} 0-1

